      <h2>Practical tips from the community</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Serve it cold and thin.</strong> More liquid, more ice. Thick shakes are the enemy of a GLP-1 stomach.</li>
        <li><strong>Sip, don't chug.</strong> Downing 400ml in one go on slowed stomach emptying is a recipe for discomfort. Treat it like a drink to nurse over half an hour.</li>
        <li><strong>Front-load your day.</strong> Many users find appetite is least bad in the morning. A clear whey with breakfast banks 20g before the day gets difficult.</li>
        <li><strong>Rotate flavours.</strong> Flavour fatigue is real when a powder becomes a daily fixture. Buy small bags until you know what you can face repeatedly.</li>
        <li><strong>Don't replace every meal.</strong> Powder fills the gap between what you can eat and what you need — it isn't a licence to stop eating protein-rich food. Whole food brings fibre, micronutrients and chewing, all of which matter.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Medical note:</strong> If you have kidney disease or have been given specific protein guidance by your care team, follow that advice rather than general targets. And if you're struggling to keep any food or fluid down on a GLP-1, that's a conversation for your prescriber, not a supplement problem.
      </div>

      <h2>Powder vs ready-made shakes: the maths</h2>
      <p>A 1kg bag of clear whey at £25–£30 gives you roughly 40 servings — about 70p each. Ready-made options like Ufit or Grenade run £2–£2.50 a bottle. If you're using one a day, powder saves £40–£50 a month, which over a year is a decent chunk of a month's medication cost. Ready-made still earns its place for handbags, desks and travel; we've ranked the best of those separately in our <a href="/food-protein-shakes-ranked">protein shakes guide</a>.</p>

      <h2>The bottom line</h2>
      <p>The best protein powder on a GLP-1 is the one you'll actually drink on a nauseous Tuesday. For most people that's a clear whey or a light plant blend, 20–30g a serving, mixed thinner than the tub suggests, sipped slowly. Everything else — brand, flavour, price — is preference.</p>
